WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.020
Glad you're here, welcome to Models and Agents episode 107 for July 10th, 2026.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.800
Your daily briefing on the AI models and agents that are changing everything.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.540
And no, not those kinds of models and agents, let's get into it.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:24.260
GPT 5.6 ships with new multi-agent and programmatic tool calling APIs, plus major cost-pertask gains

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.700
that directly affect what production agents can sustain today.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:37.040
Open AI released GPT 5.6 with expanded API support for programmatic tool calling and multi-agent orchestration.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:43.980
The release includes three new base models, along with 18 pelican variants tied to six reasoning levels.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:50.440
Explicit cost improvements target the sole variant to address enterprise dollars per task concerns,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.220
with Terra and Luna also part of the efficiency push.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:02.340
Sam Altman described it as the best model open AI has produced and pointed to a detailed accompanying blog post.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:09.680
The new capabilities build on the existing codex core that powers the work product now shipping to paid plans.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:18.000
Developers working on agent systems can now call the updated endpoints to orchestrate multiple agents without custom scaffolding.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.300
This release moves forward the capability gains versus cost trajectory question tracked since the prior episode,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:31.460
while also targeting reliability of long horizon agents through direct API support.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:39.140
Open AI also rolled out chat GPT work to pro enterprise and edu users on web and mobile.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.080
Builders should test the new tool calling endpoints this week for long horizon agent workloads,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.800
where token cost has been the blocker.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.220
Google Research, Google Deep Mind, and collaborators released sensor FM,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:01.300
a wearable health foundation model, pre-trained on more than one trillion minutes of unlabeled sensor data from five million participants.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:11.000
The model uses a VIT-1D mast autoencoder backbone and shows frozen embeddings plus a PCA-50 linear probe,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.640
beating feature engineered baselines on 34 of 35 tasks.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.140
An agentic classroom searched 30,516 prediction heads during development.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.320
The work demonstrates co-scaling results across four model sizes and four data volumes.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.120
Builders focused on time series health signals should test the released embeddings this week.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:41.700
A new preference data set called plural was introduced containing roughly 500,000 synthetic preference triplets,

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.740
drawn from the integrated value survey across 20 countries.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:53.220
The two stage generation pipeline converts nationally representative survey responses into realistic scenarios

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.900
while preserving cross country value differences.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:03.960
Training on plural reduced mean absolute error by up to 27.7% relative to strong baselines

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.200
when steering models toward target cultural profiles.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:19.600
Blind human evaluation with 176 evaluators in India, Brazil, and Japan confirmed that plural aligned responses were judged more representative of national values.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.420
The data set is available on hugging phase for teams working on pluralistic alignment.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:34.040
Researchers released a spawn stereo, a Spanish language stereotype data set spanning multiple countries in Europe and Latin America,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.180
built via a human LLM collaborative annotation framework.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:44.640
LLM's generated candidate stereotypes that in-culture annotators then validated,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:51.280
surfacing both documented and culturally specific biases absent from English-centric resources.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:04:00.340
Evaluation of Spanish supporting LLMs on the data set showed significant variation in stereotypical behavior across countries.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:07.780
The framework is positioned as adaptable to other languages and regions for scalable multi-lingual bias benchmarks.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:24.100
Holographic neural PCFG or HAL PCFG was proposed as a new parameterization that recasts PCFG rule scoring as algebraic relation modeling over torus constrained embeddings using holographic embeddings.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:33.960
It achieved state-of-the-art unsupervised parsing performance in six languages while cutting rule scoring parameters by 99.94%,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.420
relative to the neural PCFG baseline and training more stably.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:47.340
The model can parse Japanese directly from characters without morphological segmentation while retaining nearly the same morpheme level performance.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:57.500
Rabiant released Lingbot World Infinity, a 14-billion-parameter causal video generation model that functions as an interactive world simulator.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:04.940
It uses a mixture of bidirectional and autoregressive attention mask plus distribution matching distillation.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:13.540
A director pilot, a gentick harness wraps the generator so a VLM proposes events and the diffusion transformer renders them,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:18.460
supporting a single 60-minute uninterrupted session across 20 scenarios.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:28.820
The release includes one checkpoint and a 480p reference script under a non-commercial CC by northside slash SA 4.0 license.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:36.520
DeepSearch Evolve introduces a self-distillation framework for web agents built on DeepSearch Times World.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:44.980
A deterministic environment with 420,000 multi-hop QA tasks and reproducible search and page reading tools.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:54.100
The method iteratively performs trajectory generation, filtering, data mixing, and fine-tuning without distillation from stronger models.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:06:07.960
DeepSearch, DashWorld, Dash9b reaches 31.2% on BrowseCompt, 61.5% on GAIA, and 93.4% on hotpot QA.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:16.580
The environment, training pool, validation set, model, and code will be released to support self-improving long horizon agents.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:30.560
MAST is a multi-agent pipeline that decomposes zero-shot aspect sentiment triplet extraction into four sequential stages handled by specialized agents with explicit conditioning on prior outputs.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:38.060
It requires no training data or in-domain demonstrations and generalizes across backbones and data sets.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:51.780
On four AST benchmarks, it substantially outperforms zero-shot and chain-of-explanning layer-layer male-grin, yelking baselines, under the same backbone while narrowing the gap to fully supervised methods.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:07:01.340
Hallucination's self-play bootstraps are reinforced hallucination detector by pitting it against an evolved generator initialized from the same base model.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:16.860
The detector is first fine-tuned on human-label data, then used as a reward model via RLAIF to train the generator, which in turn synthesizes harder examples for the detector through rule-based reinforcement learning.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:27.840
Experiments on RAG truth and two model families show the small LLM can progressively match or exceed advanced LLMs without external supervision.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:43.560
Koala provides a framework for contextual biasing in speech-augmented language models by mapping SLM latent representations into a discriminative space that quantifies matching intensity between audio segments and candidate entities.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:53.020
It addresses training collapse on multi-target utterances and delivers superior performance across varying biasing-list scales on library speech.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:08:00.480
The approach targets complex multi-entity scenarios where context window limits make entity selection critical.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:15.220
Tail-aware credit calibration or taco was introduced to fix positive credit contamination in critic-free RL for LLMs, where low probability tail tokens receive the same positive credit as plausible ones.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:25.060
The method computes a tail-risk score from local generation context and uses it to modulate positive credit without removing gradients entirely.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:36.620
Experiments across three LLMs and eight benchmarks show consistent gains over GRPO style baselines plus improved training stability for long horizon RL.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:47.440
Cobart applies prefix control tokens with Bart fine-tuning for ad headline generation, allowing length control across formats while optimizing click-through rate.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:57.560
It reports a 25.82% increase in Ruzla and 5.82% estimated CTR lift over prior strong baselines.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:03.360
The method is described as flexible for other architectures and optimization criteria.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:10.460
Okay, let's pop the hood on tail-aware credit assignment in reinforcement learning for LLMs.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:18.180
Everyone talks about reinforcement learning on LLMs as if uniform advantage broadcasting is a neutral default.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:30.840
In practice, the design creates positive credit contamination because every token in a trajectory receives the identical scalar regardless of how surprising or contextually unsupported it was.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:43.840
The core insight starts with the observation that tail tokens, those falling into the low probability region of the current policy, are disproportionately likely to be errors rather than useful exploration.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:56.760
Taco therefore computes a local tail-risk score that conditions on the preceding tokens in the same generation then scales the positive credit applied to those tokens without zeroing their gradients.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:06.120
This preserves the possibility that genuinely rare but correct patterns accumulate reinforcement while incidental noise is progressively damped.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:19.380
The practical trade-off is modest extra compute for the risk scoring step versus the stability gains that let training continue without the variance spikes that usually force early stopping on long horizon tasks.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:34.520
When your RL runs show high variance across repeated trajectories on the same prompt or when you notice the model reinforcing fluent but factually broken reasoning, the tail calibration step is the liver worth adding first.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:42.300
Uniform credit remains acceptable only for short high certainty tasks where tail errors are rare by construction.

